AWPTCM-T11773 (1.0)  Testing SSM Address Range
Run date : 2026-09-10      Bench : tb470 (IE520)
Software : IE520-awplus_main-20260905-1714.rel  (AlliedWare Plus, build 09/04/26; bootloader pauld)
Result   : PASS
================================================================================

RESULT
  Groups inside and outside the SSM address range are handled correctly:
    INSIDE the SSM range (user range 239.100.0.0/16):
      - (S,G) source-specific: joined source delivered, UNJOINED source dropped
            joined S1  -> 19/20      unjoined S2 -> 0/20  ((S2,G) has empty oif)
      - (*,G) ASM join: NO shared tree is built -> arbitrary source dropped
            (*,G) join, arbitrary source S3 -> 0/20
    OUTSIDE the SSM range (239.202.0.40, ASM):
      - (*,G) ASM join builds an RP shared tree -> arbitrary source delivered
            (*,G) join, arbitrary source S3 -> 19/20
  => in-range is source-specific ONLY (no ASM shared tree); out-of-range is normal
     ASM.  Groups in/out of the range are treated correctly.               => PASS.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BENCH / TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DUT / PIM-SSM router : u4  S/N 264A23052  MAC 84e3.2787.09c0
      RP (for ASM)     : loopback 10.10.99.1
      SSM range        : access-list 40 permit 239.100.0.0 0.0.255.255 ; ip pim ssm range 40
  Host (one NIC, tagged sub-ifs on u4 port1.0.2):
      source   : eth2.81  vlan81  - sends from 10.10.81.20 (S1), .21 (S2), .30 (S3)
      receiver : eth2.82  10.10.82.20  (vlan82)
  Groups: in-range 239.100.0.20 / 239.100.0.40 ; out-of-range 239.202.0.40

METHOD / SCOPE
  No IXIA.  IGMPv3 CHANGE_TO_INCLUDE {S} (SSM) and IGMPv2 (*,G) (ASM) joins are sent
  with scapy.  For each case the receiver is sniffed while a source transmits, and
  the switch mroute/igmp state is read.  The joined vs unjoined source, and the ASM
  in-range vs out-of-range, are the two axes that prove correct range handling.
  Functional test.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A - INSIDE the range: source-specific filtering (239.100.0.20)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### receiver joins IGMPv3 (S1=10.10.81.20, 239.100.0.20) ###
  joined   source S1=10.10.81.20 -> received: 19 of 20
  UNJOINED source S2=10.10.81.21 -> received:  0 of 20

u4# show ip mroute 239.100.0.20
(10.10.81.20, 239.100.0.20)  Incoming vlan81  Outgoing: vlan82 (1)   <-- joined S1 forwards
(10.10.81.21, 239.100.0.20)  Incoming vlan81  Outgoing: <empty>      <-- S2 has NO oif: dropped
  => inside the SSM range only the specifically-joined source is delivered; another
     source to the same group is dropped (empty outgoing list).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART B - INSIDE the range: an ASM (*,G) join builds NO shared tree (239.100.0.40)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### receiver sends an IGMPv2 (*,G) EXCLUDE join for 239.100.0.40 (in SSM range) ###
u4# show ip igmp groups 239.100.0.40 detail
  Group mode:     Exclude (Expires: 00:03:54)     <-- host asked for ASM (*,G)
  Source list is empty
  arbitrary source S3=10.10.81.30 -> received: 0 of 20
  => although the host requested (*,G), the router builds NO RP shared tree for a
     group in the SSM range, so traffic from an unspecified source is NOT delivered.
     A group in the SSM range is source-specific only - correctly NOT treated as ASM.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART C - OUTSIDE the range: ASM (*,G) works via the RP (239.202.0.40)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### receiver sends an IGMPv2 (*,G) EXCLUDE join for 239.202.0.40 (outside SSM range) ###
u4# show ip igmp groups 239.202.0.40 detail
  Group mode:     Exclude (Expires: 00:04:03)
  Source list is empty
  arbitrary source S3=10.10.81.30 -> received: 19 of 20

u4# show ip mroute 239.202.0.40
(10.10.81.30, 239.202.0.40)  Incoming vlan81  Outgoing: vlan82 (1)
  => outside the SSM range the (*,G) join is honoured as ASM: an RP shared tree is
     built and traffic from an arbitrary source is delivered.  This is the correct
     contrast to Part B - the SSM range boundary is enforced.  PASS.
